Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Bifurcation Diagram", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.941181107152174*^9, 
  3.941181110122642*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"50827f15-df0b-4730-9868-\
35517d485619"],

Cell["\<\
Similarly to in the continuous case, we can plot a bifurcation diagram, where \
we plot points the system visits against a parameter value. This will pick up \
stable points and orbits, but not any unstable points. In this example, we \
run the simulation for 2000 steps and discard the first 1000 points where the \
system is settling its transient behaviour, from there we plot the remaining \
points.
As with the spider diagram, you can observe the regions of stable values, \
orbits, chaos, and intermittent islands where they transition.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.973929015865842*^9, 3.9739291942773848`*^9}, {
  3.973929547789454*^9, 3.973929568231572*^9}, {3.9739296506507998`*^9, 
  3.973929666752796*^9}, {3.974180211871358*^9, 3.974180228078265*^9}, {
  3.97418038884037*^9, 3.97418042780847*^9}, {3.974181635577902*^9, 
  3.974181644065112*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9bd86a02-e93f-4878-b1b6-\
34f1af6ff0b3"],

